 the ℓ-set of cycles we obtain by developing this cycle on first coordinates consists of equitably coloured cycles. Indeed the last two vertices will have different colours in C and all its translates; the remaining vertices form a run of (ℓ − 1)/2 consecutive (mod ℓ) 1-vertices, and (ℓ − 3)/2 consecutive (mod ℓ) 0-vertices, starting from, in the cycle C, 0₁, ..., (ℓ − 3)/2₁ and (ℓ + 1)/2₀, ..., (ℓ − 2)₀. It can be checked that--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGraham–Häggkvist conjecture for complete bipartite hosts | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/365359/graham-haggkvist-conjecture-for-complete-bipartite-hosts)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Graham–Häggkvist conjecture for complete bipartite hosts\n\n# Graham–Häggkvist conjecture for complete bipartite hosts\n\nAbout 2 years old · traced to 2024\n\nA graph decomposes $G$G if copies of the graph form a perfect packing of $G$G, and $K_{n,n}$K n,n​ denotes the complete bipartite graph with $n$n vertices in each part.\n\nGraham–Häggkvist complete-bipartite conjecture. Any tree of order $n+1$n+1 decomposes the complete bipartite graph $K_{n,n}$K n,n​.\n\nThis follows from the stated conjecture that every tree of order $n+1$n+1 decomposes every $n$n-regular bipartite graph. The source reports approximate results and evidence, but the exact assertion remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nCristina G. Fernandes, Tássio Naia, Giovanne Santos and Maya Stein, “Packing large balanced trees into bipartite graphs”, arXiv:2410.13290 (2024).\n\nbipartite-graphsgraph-decompositionstree-packings\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.-------------------------------------------------